  "overview": {
    "text": "Area measures how much two-dimensional space a flat region covers, and it is expressed in square units such as square centimeters, not linear units such as centimeters. It distinguishes the size of a region from the length of its boundary, making it useful for estimating flooring or paint. For familiar shapes, formulas use dimensions: a rectangle’s area is length times width, and a triangle’s is half its base times its height. For curved regions, calculus can approximate area by adding thin rectangles and taking a limit. A definite integral gives signed area (above the x-axis minus below it); total area counts both portions positively. In two dimensions, integrating 1 over a region gives its area.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-6-area",
      "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/1-1-approximating-areas",
      "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/1-2-the-definite-integral",
      "https://math.libretexts.org/Courses/University_of_California_Irvine/MATH_2E%3A_Multivariable_Calculus/Chapter_14%3A_Multiple_Integration/14.2a%3A_Double_Integrals_Over_Rectangular_Regions"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Area is measured in square units; perimeter measures the boundary in linear units.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-6-area"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A rectangle’s area is length × width; a triangle’s area is ½ × base × height.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/10-6-area"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For curved regions, sums of rectangle areas can approach the exact area as the rectangles become narrower and their number increases.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/1-1-approximating-areas"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A definite integral gives signed area: area above the x-axis minus area below it. To get total area, count both portions positively.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/1-2-the-definite-integral"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A double integral of the constant function 1 over a plane region gives that region’s area.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://math.libretexts.org/Courses/University_of_California_Irvine/MATH_2E%3A_Multivariable_Calculus/Chapter_14%3A_Multiple_Integration/14.2a%3A_Double_Integrals_Over_Rectangular_Regions"
      ]
    }
  ],
